From cc77766cccf8f8232119182fd20c37db2251075d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jacob Root <me@rooty.dev>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 06:09:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 09/11] devices: run the nvgpu vhost-user frontend in a jailed
 process

A vhost-user frontend runs in crosvm's main process: the virtqueues are the
backend's. virtio-nvgpu's frontend also serves the backend's requests to
map its descriptors into the guest, and the main process is not
seccomp-confined, so with the sandbox on the nvgpu frontend now runs in a
minijail'd process of its own like every device crosvm emulates, under a
policy of its own (vhost_user_frontend_device: common_device.policy, plus
getrandom, prctl PR_SET_NAME/PR_SET_PDEATHSIG, and open/openat refused with
ENOENT -- no socket, no ioctl beyond the common vmm-swap ones). Its mapping
requests go to the main process over the device's VmMemoryRequest tube, as a
jailed virtio-gpu's do; the main process checks each again and makes it. The
child makes no KVM call and maps nothing into guest memory. Other vhost-user
types keep upstream's behaviour. keep_rds names what the child needs: its
backend socket, the backend request channel, the crash tube and the memory
tube.

A virtio device's control tube (its PCI transport's, whichever process that
is in) no longer takes HotPlugVfioCommand: only power management events come
that way, and a compromised device process could otherwise have the main
process add a host VFIO device to the VM. Hot-plug ports keep theirs
(AnyControlTube::DeviceNoHotplug).

The test forks a real frontend under the embedded policy, keeping only its
keep_rds and what every device process is handed, against a fake backend
process and a fake main process: it reads the config, activates, forwards
window mappings and a UVM pool, refuses a pool outside the band itself, and
resets, withdrawing both. A second test checks the policy stops a socket, an
ioctl, and refuses an open. VmRequester is exported with test-util for it.

run --help names the nvgpu-uvm-aperture, for a launcher to detect.

